How would you go about implementing a distributed system in Swift for real-time collaborative editing of a single document? Can you provide a code snippet that uses message-passing protocols to ensure data consistency and fault tolerance?
Here is a sample code snippet that uses Apple's Multipeer Connectivity framework to achieve real-time collaboration:
```swift
import MultipeerConnectivity
class CollaborativeEditor {
    var peers: [MCPeerID] = []
    var session: MCSession?
    var browser: MCNearbyServiceBrowser?
    var advertiser: MCNearbyServiceAdvertiser?
    func setup() {
        let peerID = MCPeerID(displayName: UIDevice.current.name)
        session = MCSession(peer: peerID, securityIdentity: nil, encryptionPreference: .required)
        session?.delegate = self
        browser = MCNearbyServiceBrowser(peer: peerID, serviceType: "collaborative-editing")
        browser?.delegate = self
        browser?.startBrowsingForPeers()
        advertiser = MCNearbyServiceAdvertiser(peer: peerID, discoveryInfo: nil, serviceType: "collaborative-editing")
        advertiser?.delegate = self
        advertiser?.startAdvertisingPeer()
    }
    func send(data: Data) {
        try? session?.send(data, toPeers: peers, with: .reliable)
    }
}
extension CollaborativeEditor: MCSessionDelegate {
    func session(_ session: MCSession, peer peerID: MCPeerID, didChange state: MCSessionState) {
        switch state {
        case .connected:
            peers.append(peerID)
        case .notConnected:
            if let index = peers.firstIndex(of: peerID) {
                peers.remove(at: index)
            }
        default:
            break
        }
    }
    func session(_ session: MCSession, didReceive data: Data, fromPeer peerID: MCPeerID) {
        // Handle incoming data from peers
    }
}
extension CollaborativeEditor: MCNearbyServiceBrowserDelegate {
    func browser(_ browser: MCNearbyServiceBrowser, foundPeer peerID: MCPeerID, withDiscoveryInfo info: [String : String]?) {
        browser.invitePeer(peerID, to: session!, withContext: nil, timeout: 10)
    }
    func browser(_ browser: MCNearbyServiceBrowser, lostPeer peerID: MCPeerID) {
        if let index = peers.firstIndex(of: peerID) {
            peers.remove(at: index)
        }
    }
}
extension CollaborativeEditor: MCNearbyServiceAdvertiserDelegate {
    func advertiser(_ advertiser: MCNearbyServiceAdvertiser, didReceiveInvitationFromPeer peerID: MCPeerID, withContext context: Data?, invitationHandler: @escaping (Bool, MCSession?) -> Void) {
        invitationHandler(true, session!)
    }
}
```
This code sets up a `CollaborativeEditor` class that uses the Multipeer Connectivity framework to establish a peer-to-peer network for real-time collaboration. The `setup()` function initializes the `MCSession` and starts browsing and advertising for peers. The `send(data:)` function sends data to all connected peers, and the `MCSessionDelegate` methods handle changes in peer connection status and incoming data. The `MCNearbyServiceBrowserDelegate` and `MCNearbyServiceAdvertiserDelegate` methods handle the discovery and invitation of nearby peers.
To ensure data consistency and fault tolerance, you can implement additional message-passing protocols such as message ordering, message acks, and conflict resolution.

