To implement a formal/informal classifier for sentences in your web application using JavaScript, you can use natural language processing libraries like Natural, Compromise, or NLP.js. These libraries have pre-trained models that can classify sentences based on their formality.
Here's an example using the Natural library:
```javascript
const natural = require('natural');
const tokenizer = new natural.WordTokenizer();
// Load the pre-trained model
const classifier = new natural.BayesClassifier();
classifier.load('formality_classifier.json', null, function(err, classifier) {
  if (err) {
    console.log('Error loading classifier:', err);
  } else {
    // Classify a sentence
    const sentence = 'Sup, how\'s it going?';
    const tokens = tokenizer.tokenize(sentence.toLowerCase());
    const classification = classifier.classify(tokens);
    console.log('Classification:', classification);
  }
});
```
In this example, we load a pre-trained classifier from a JSON file called `formality_classifier.json`. We then tokenize the input sentence using the `WordTokenizer` from the Natural library. Finally, we use the `classify` method of the classifier to classify the sentence as either formal or informal.
To train your own classifier, you'll need to provide a dataset of labeled sentences (i.e., sentences labeled as formal or informal). You can then use the `addDocument` method of the classifier to add each sentence to the training set, and the `train` method to train the classifier. Here's an example:
```javascript
// Create a new classifier
const classifier = new natural.BayesClassifier();
// Add labeled sentences to the training set
classifier.addDocument('Good morning, sir.', 'formal');
classifier.addDocument('Hey, what\'s up?', 'informal');
classifier.addDocument('Excuse me, may I have your attention, please?', 'formal');
classifier.addDocument('Yo, listen up!', 'informal');
// Train the classifier
classifier.train();
// Save the trained classifier to a JSON file
classifier.save('formality_classifier.json');
```
In this example, we add four labeled sentences to the training set and train the classifier using the `train` method. We then save the trained classifier to a JSON file called `formality_classifier.json`. You can add more labeled sentences to the training set to improve the accuracy of the classifier.
Using the classifier on the example sentence "Sup, how's it going?" would result in a classification of "informal".

Disruptive technologies can be leveraged in the field of Scala programming to improve performance, scalability, and efficiency. One example of implementing a disruptive technology in Scala code is by using Akka, an open-source toolkit and runtime for building highly concurrent, distributed, and fault-tolerant systems.
Akka is built on the Actor model, which allows for lightweight, asynchronous, and message-driven communication between actors. This makes it ideal for building scalable and fault-tolerant systems that can handle high levels of concurrency.
Here's an example of how Akka can be used in Scala code:
```
import akka.actor.{Actor, ActorSystem, Props}
class MyActor extends Actor {
  def receive = {
    case message: String => println(s"Received message: $message")
  }
}
object Main extends App {
  val system = ActorSystem("MySystem")
  val myActor = system.actorOf(Props[MyActor], "MyActor")
  myActor ! "Hello, Akka!"
}
```
In this example, we create an actor system and a `MyActor` class that extends the `Actor` trait. The `MyActor` class defines a `receive` method that handles incoming messages. We then create an instance of `MyActor` and send it a message using the `!` operator.
Akka allows us to build highly scalable and fault-tolerant systems by distributing work across multiple nodes and handling failures gracefully. By leveraging disruptive technologies like Akka, we can build more efficient and robust systems in Scala programming.

