First, to implement Ohm's law in Java, you can use the following formula:
V = I * R
Where V is the voltage, I is the current, and R is the resistance. Here's an example Java program that calculates the power dissipation of a resistor given its resistance value and the current flowing through it:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;
public class PowerDissipation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter the resistance value in ohms: ");
        double resistance = input.nextDouble();
        System.out.print("Enter the current flowing through the resistor in amperes: ");
        double current = input.nextDouble();
        double power = Math.pow(current, 2) * resistance;
        System.out.println("The power dissipation of the resistor is " + power + " watts.");
    }
}
```
Regarding the differences between series and parallel circuits, in a series circuit, the components are connected one after the other, so the same current flows through each component, and the voltage is divided between them. In a parallel circuit, the components are connected in parallel, so the voltage is the same across each component, and the current is divided between them. Here are some example Java programs for each configuration:
Series circuit:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;
public class SeriesCircuit {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter the total voltage in volts: ");
        double voltage = input.nextDouble();
        System.out.print("Enter the resistance of the first component in ohms: ");
        double resistance1 = input.nextDouble();
        System.out.print("Enter the resistance of the second component in ohms: ");
        double resistance2 = input.nextDouble();
        double current = voltage / (resistance1 + resistance2);
        double voltage1 = current * resistance1;
        double voltage2 = current * resistance2;
        System.out.println("The current flowing through the circuit is " + current + " amperes.");
        System.out.println("The voltage across the first component is " + voltage1 + " volts.");
        System.out.println("The voltage across the second component is " + voltage2 + " volts.");
    }
}
```
Parallel circuit:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;
public class ParallelCircuit {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter the total current in amperes: ");
        double current = input.nextDouble();
        System.out.print("Enter the resistance of the first component in ohms: ");
        double resistance1 = input.nextDouble();
        System.out.print("Enter the resistance of the second component in ohms: ");
        double resistance2 = input.nextDouble();
        double voltage1 = current * resistance1;
        double voltage2 = current * resistance2;
        double totalResistance = (resistance1 * resistance2) / (resistance1 + resistance2);
        double totalCurrent = voltage1 / totalResistance;
        double totalVoltage = voltage1 + voltage2;
        System.out.println("The total current flowing through the circuit is " + totalCurrent + " amperes.");
        System.out.println("The voltage across the first component is " + voltage1 + " volts.");
        System.out.println("The voltage across the second component is " + voltage2 + " volts.");
        System.out.println("The total voltage across the circuit is " + totalVoltage + " volts.");
    }
}
```
Regarding capacitance and inductance, these are properties of components that affect the behavior of electrical circuits. Capacitance is the ability of a component to store electrical charge, while inductance is the ability to store magnetic energy. To simulate their effects using Java code, you can use circuit simulation libraries like CircuitSim or QUCS. Here's an example Java program that models the charging and discharging of a capacitor in a simple RC circuit:
```java
import org.opensourcephysics.display.Trail;
import org.opensourcephysics.frames.PlotFrame;
public class RCircuit {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double R = 1000; // resistance in ohms
        double C = 1e-6; // capacitance in farads
        double V0 = 5; // initial voltage in volts
        double tMax = 0.1; // simulation time in seconds
        double dt = 1e-5; // time step in seconds
        double q = C * V0; // initial charge on the capacitor
        double i = 0; // initial current
        PlotFrame frame = new PlotFrame("Time (s)", "Voltage (V)", "Capacitor Charging and Discharging");
        Trail voltageTrail = new Trail();
        Trail currentTrail = new Trail();
        frame.setPreferredMinMaxX(0, tMax);
        frame.setPreferredMinMaxY(-V0, V0);
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(3);
        frame.setVisible(true);
        for (double t = 0; t <= tMax; t += dt) {
            double V = V0 * (1 - Math.exp(-t / (R * C))); // voltage across the capacitor
            double dQ = i * dt; // change in charge
            double dI = -V / R * dt; // change in current
            q += dQ; // update charge
            i += dI; // update current
            voltageTrail.addPoint(t, V);
            currentTrail.addPoint(t, i);
        }
        frame.addDrawable(voltageTrail);
        frame.addDrawable(currentTrail);
    }
}
```
