You can use the Natural Language Processing (NLP) library in C# called Stanford.NLP to analyze the parts of speech in a sentence. To remove punctuation marks, you can use the regular expressions in C#. Here's a sample code that demonstrates how to analyze the parts of speech and remove punctuation marks in a sentence:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using edu.stanford.nlp.ling;
using edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline;
using edu.stanford.nlp.util;
namespace NLPDemo
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Create a new pipeline with default settings
            var pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP();
            // Define the text to analyze
            var text = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.";
            // Remove punctuation marks
            text = Regex.Replace(text, @"[^\w\s]", "");
            // Create a new annotation object
            var annotation = new Annotation(text);
            // Annotate the text
            pipeline.annotate(annotation);
            // Get the list of tokens
            var tokens = annotation.get(typeof(CoreAnnotations.TokensAnnotation));
            // Loop through each token and output its part of speech
            foreach (CoreLabel token in tokens)
            {
                Console.WriteLine("{0}: {1}", token.originalText(), token.get(typeof(CoreAnnotations.PartOfSpeechAnnotation)));
            }
            Console.ReadLine();
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses the StanfordCoreNLP library to create a pipeline for analyzing the text. It then removes any punctuation marks using regular expressions. After that, it creates an annotation object for the text and annotates it using the pipeline. Finally, it loops through each token in the text and outputs its part of speech.

To efficiently organize and analyze customer records for the past year using MATLAB, we can use a combination of data structures and functions to create a data schema that is optimized for ease of retrieval and analysis. Here is a sample code that demonstrates the proposed data organization strategy:
```matlab
% Define data schema
customerRecord = struct('name', '', 'address', '', 'phone', '', 'email', '', 'purchases', []);
purchaseRecord = struct('date', '', 'product', '', 'price', '');
% Load customer data
load('customerData.mat'); % Assumes customerData.mat contains a struct array with fields: name, address, phone, email, purchases
% Initialize data structures
numCustomers = length(customerData);
customerRecords = repmat(customerRecord, numCustomers, 1);
purchaseRecords = [];
% Populate customer records
for i = 1:numCustomers
    customerRecords(i).name = customerData(i).name;
    customerRecords(i).address = customerData(i).address;
    customerRecords(i).phone = customerData(i).phone;
    customerRecords(i).email = customerData(i).email;

    % Populate purchase records for customer
    numPurchases = length(customerData(i).purchases);
    for j = 1:numPurchases
        purchaseRecord.date = customerData(i).purchases(j).date;
        purchaseRecord.product = customerData(i).purchases(j).product;
        purchaseRecord.price = customerData(i).purchases(j).price;
        purchaseRecords = [purchaseRecords; purchaseRecord];
    end

    % Add purchase records to customer record
    customerRecords(i).purchases = purchaseRecords(end-numPurchases+1:end);
end
% Analyze customer and purchase data
% Example 1: Calculate total revenue for the past year
totalRevenue = sum([purchaseRecords.price]);
% Example 2: Find customers who purchased a specific product
product = 'iPhone';
customers = {customerRecords([customerRecords.purchases].product) == product}.name;
```
In this sample code, we define a data schema that consists of two struct types: `customerRecord` and `purchaseRecord`. `customerRecord` contains fields for the customer's name, address, phone, email, and a list of purchases. `purchaseRecord` contains fields for the purchase date, product, and price.
We then load the customer data and initialize two data structures: `customerRecords` and `purchaseRecords`. We populate `customerRecords` by iterating through each customer in the `customerData` struct array and copying over the relevant fields. We also populate `purchaseRecords` by iterating through each purchase in each customer's `purchases` field and appending it to the end of the `purchaseRecords` array. We then add the relevant purchases to each customer's `purchases` field in the `customerRecords` array.
Finally, we demonstrate two examples of how to analyze the customer and purchase data. In the first example, we calculate the total revenue for the past year by summing up the `price` field of all `purchaseRecords`. In the second example, we find all customers who purchased a specific product by filtering the `customerRecords` array based on the `product` field of each purchase in each customer's `purchases` field.

