To detect and highlight instances of a specific word followed by a hyperlink in a paragraph and change the hyperlink to a button, you can use regular expressions to search for the word and then use the Java Swing library to replace the hyperlink with a button. Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates how to do this:
```java
import java.awt.Color;
import java.awt.event.ActionEvent;
import java.awt.event.ActionListener;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import javax.swing.JButton;
import javax.swing.JFrame;
import javax.swing.JPanel;
import javax.swing.JScrollPane;
import javax.swing.JTextArea;
import javax.swing.text.BadLocationException;
import javax.swing.text.DefaultHighlighter;
import javax.swing.text.Highlighter;
import javax.swing.text.Highlighter.HighlightPainter;
import javax.swing.text.JTextComponent;
public class HighlightHyperlink {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        JFrame frame = new JFrame("Highlight Hyperlink");
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        JPanel panel = new JPanel();
        JTextArea textArea = new JTextArea("This is a paragraph with a hyperlink to Google: https://www.google.com. This is another paragraph with a hyperlink to Yahoo: https://www.yahoo.com.");
        JScrollPane scrollPane = new JScrollPane(textArea);
        panel.add(scrollPane);
        JButton highlightButton = new JButton("Highlight Links");
        highlightButton.addActionListener(new ActionListener() {
            @Override
            public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {
                String patternString = "\\b(Google|Yahoo)\\b\\s*:\\s*https?://\\S+";
                Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile(patternString);
                Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(textArea.getText());
                Highlighter highlighter = textArea.getHighlighter();
                HighlightPainter painter = new DefaultHighlighter.DefaultHighlightPainter(Color.YELLOW);
                while (matcher.find()) {
                    int start = matcher.start();
                    int end = matcher.end();
                    try {
                        highlighter.addHighlight(start, end, painter);
                        String linkText = textArea.getText(start, end - start);
                        JButton linkButton = new JButton(linkText);
                        linkButton.addActionListener(new ActionListener() {
                            @Override
                            public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {
                                System.out.println("Button clicked: " + linkText);
                            }
                        });
                        textArea.replaceRange("", start, end);
                        textArea.insertComponent(linkButton);
                    } catch (BadLocationException ex) {
                        ex.printStackTrace();
                    }
                }
            }
        });
        panel.add(highlightButton);
        frame.getContentPane().add(panel);
        frame.pack();
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }
}
```
In this code, we first create a JTextArea with some sample text containing two hyperlinks to Google and Yahoo. We then create a JButton that will initiate the highlighting and replacement of hyperlinks with buttons. When the button is clicked, we use a regular expression to search for instances of the words "Google" or "Yahoo" followed by a hyperlink. We then use the Java Swing Highlighter API to highlight these instances in the JTextArea. Finally, we replace each hyperlink with a JButton that has the same text as the hyperlink, and add an ActionListener to the button that will print a message to the console when the button is clicked.
Note that this code only works for hyperlinks that are formatted with the protocol (http or https) and the domain name (e.g. google.com). If the hyperlink is formatted differently (e.g. with a subdirectory or query parameters), the regular expression will need to be updated accordingly. Also note that this code only modifies the text in the JTextArea, so if the text is also displayed in other components or classes, those components will need to be updated separately.

