To utilize MATLAB for stock market analysis and prediction, you can use various techniques such as time series analysis, machine learning algorithms, and statistical modeling. These techniques can be used to identify patterns and trends in stock market data, and make predictions based on historical data.
Here are some examples of MATLAB code that can be used to implement investment strategies and manage portfolios based on stock market predictions:
1. Moving Average Crossover Strategy:
```
% Load stock data
data = readtable('stock_data.csv');
% Calculate 50-day and 200-day moving averages
ma50 = movmean(data.Close, [49 0]);
ma200 = movmean(data.Close, [199 0]);
% Determine buy and sell signals based on moving average crossovers
buySignal = (ma50 > ma200) & (ma50 < [ma50(2:end); NaN]);
sellSignal = (ma50 < ma200) & (ma50 > [ma50(2:end); NaN]);
% Create portfolio based on buy and sell signals
portfolio = zeros(size(data,1),1);
portfolio(buySignal) = 1;
portfolio(sellSignal) = -1;
% Calculate portfolio returns
returns = portfolio .* [0; diff(data.Close)];
cumulativeReturns = cumsum(returns);
% Plot portfolio returns
plot(data.Date, cumulativeReturns);
```
2. Support Vector Machine (SVM) Strategy:
```
% Load stock data
data = readtable('stock_data.csv');
% Split data into training and testing sets
trainData = data(1:floor(end/2),:);
testData = data(floor(end/2)+1:end,:);
% Train SVM model on training data
Xtrain = table2array(trainData(:,2:end));
Ytrain = sign(diff(trainData.Close));
svmModel = fitcsvm(Xtrain, Ytrain);
% Make predictions on testing data using SVM model
Xtest = table2array(testData(:,2:end));
predictedY = predict(svmModel, Xtest);
% Determine buy and sell signals based on SVM predictions
buySignal = (predictedY > 0);
sellSignal = (predictedY < 0);
% Create portfolio based on buy and sell signals
portfolio = zeros(size(testData,1),1);
portfolio(buySignal) = 1;
portfolio(sellSignal) = -1;
% Calculate portfolio returns
returns = portfolio .* [0; diff(testData.Close)];
cumulativeReturns = cumsum(returns);
% Plot portfolio returns
plot(testData.Date, cumulativeReturns);
```
